1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:06,353
In this tutorial we install Cache &
Carry on a fresh server, step by step.

2
00:00:06,453 --> 00:00:10,224
Cache & Carry is a package
cache for your local network.

3
00:00:11,074 --> 00:00:18,338
The first machine downloads a package from the Internet. Cache & Carry
stores it, and every other machine gets it straight from the LAN.

4
00:00:18,438 --> 00:00:25,386
Supported today: APT for Debian, Ubuntu and
Proxmox, and Pacman for Arch Linux and CachyOS.

5
00:00:25,486 --> 00:00:36,801
The hardware can be small: one core and 256 MB of RAM are the minimum, two cores
and one GB are comfortable. Plus disk space for everything you want to cache.

6
00:00:36,901 --> 00:00:43,281
At the end you have a running service, a dashboard and two
Debian clients that get their packages from your cache.

7
00:00:45,631 --> 00:00:55,544
We take the recommended route: an unprivileged Debian 13 container on
Proxmox. A normal Debian or Ubuntu server with systemd works the same way.

8
00:00:55,644 --> 00:01:03,241
On the Proxmox host we create the container: two cores,
one GB of RAM, eight GB of storage and a fixed IP address.

9
00:01:03,341 --> 00:01:13,045
Proxmox warns that systemd 257 may need nesting. Cache & Carry does
not: the service was tested exactly like this, without nesting.

10
00:01:13,795 --> 00:01:22,454
In the container's console we check the system: Debian 13,
architecture amd64. That decides which release archive we need.

11
00:01:22,554 --> 00:01:35,169
We will need the address 192.168.1.41 for the clients later. Two
cores, one GB of RAM, and enough space for the cache under /var/lib.

12
00:01:35,269 --> 00:01:41,073
We work as root here. On a normal server,
put sudo in front of the install commands.

13
00:01:43,423 --> 00:01:50,084
Now we download the official release 0.2.1
from GitHub, together with the checksum file.

14
00:01:50,184 --> 00:01:54,764
sha256sum confirms: the
archive is unchanged.

15
00:01:54,864 --> 00:02:01,708
We unpack it. It contains the program, the systemd
unit, an example configuration and the install script.

16
00:02:02,458 --> 00:02:10,093
The script creates the system user cachencarry, installs the program to
/usr/local/bin and creates /etc/cachencarry and /var/lib/cachencarry.

17
00:02:10,193 --> 00:02:14,456
Then it checks the configuration and
starts the hardened systemd service.

18
00:02:15,388 --> 00:02:21,807
systemctl shows: the cachencarry service is
active and running. The health check answers ok.

19
00:02:24,158 --> 00:02:28,167
Now open the server's address
on port 8080 in your browser.

20
00:02:28,267 --> 00:02:37,236
On the first visit you create the administrator account. So that only someone
with access to the server can do this, setup asks for a one-time setup token.

21
00:02:37,986 --> 00:02:43,192
You read the token on the server with this
command. It is blurred in this video.

22
00:02:44,186 --> 00:02:49,741
User name, a password with at least ten characters,
paste the token, and create the account.

23
00:02:54,286 --> 00:02:58,666
Done. The dashboard is still empty
because no client uses the cache yet.

24
00:03:01,016 --> 00:03:06,611
Now the first client, a Debian 13 machine.
Here you see its original package source.

25
00:03:07,516 --> 00:03:18,224
We back up the file and replace it with the version from the documentation. Instead
of deb.debian.org it points to the cache on port 8080 with the path apt/debian.

26
00:03:18,324 --> 00:03:26,194
The Signed-By line stays. apt still checks the signatures
itself. Cache & Carry passes the files through unchanged.

27
00:03:26,846 --> 00:03:32,151
The dashboard also shows the matching configuration
for every repository under Cache Modules.

28
00:03:33,246 --> 00:03:37,339
Now apt update. The package lists
already come through the cache.

29
00:03:37,439 --> 00:03:46,404
Then we install curl. The cache is still empty, so Cache & Carry fetches
every package once from the Debian mirror. Those are the MISS entries.

30
00:03:47,054 --> 00:03:55,458
Arch Linux and CachyOS work the same way through
the mirrorlist. The lines are in docs/CLIENTS.md.

31
00:03:57,808 --> 00:04:06,551
Now the exciting part: a second client with the same configuration.
Here it runs on the same PC, so the dashboard shows the same address.

32
00:04:07,201 --> 00:04:10,088
apt update and the same installation.

33
00:04:10,188 --> 00:04:19,957
This time everything comes from the cache. For the first client the
download took three seconds, now zero seconds, at 252 MB per second.

34
00:04:20,731 --> 00:04:27,055
Live Traffic shows the difference: at the top the HIT entries
of the second client, below them the MISS entries of the first.

35
00:04:27,801 --> 00:04:34,409
On the command line you check it with curl and the X-Cache
header. On the very first request it says MISS, afterwards HIT.

36
00:04:35,059 --> 00:04:40,813
Statistics count the bytes that were really transferred:
what came from upstream and what the cache saved.

37
00:04:43,189 --> 00:04:46,244
Your cache is running. Here
is where to find everything.

38
00:04:46,344 --> 00:04:53,778
The configuration is in /etc/cachencarry/config.yaml, the
cache in /var/lib/cachencarry, and journalctl shows the logs.

39
00:04:54,819 --> 00:05:02,496
In the dashboard, stored packages are under Storage. Under
Settings you limit cache size and bandwidth, without a restart.

40
00:05:02,596 --> 00:05:07,811
Client instructions are under Cache
Modules and in the documentation on GitHub.

41
00:05:08,789 --> 00:05:12,262
And that's it. Your own package
warehouse on your network.

42
00:05:12,362 --> 00:05:19,021
You can find the complete source code for free on GitHub.
If you like Cache & Carry, feel free to leave a star.
